::ALG2_U05_L01_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=x+2 and y=3x-2.
{
=A. (2,4)#Correct. Set x+2=3x-2, so x=2 and y=4.
~B. (1,3)#This satisfies the first equation but not the second.
~C. (2,2)#This has the correct x but wrong y.
~D. (4,2)#This reverses the relationship.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=2x+5 and y=-x-1.
{
~A. (2,9)#This satisfies only the first equation.
=B. (-2,1)#Correct. Set 2x+5=-x-1, so x=-2 and y=1.
~C. (-1,3)#This does not satisfy the second equation.
~D. (1,-2)#This does not satisfy either equation.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve x+y=6 and x-y=2.
{
~A. (2,4)#This reverses the coordinates and fails x-y=2.
~B. (3,3)#This satisfies x+y=6 but not x-y=2.
=C. (4,2)#Correct. Adding equations gives 2x=8, so x=4 and y=2.
~D. (5,1)#This gives x-y=4.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve 2x-y=1 and x+y=5.
{
~A. (3,2)#This reverses the solution and fails the first equation.
~B. (1,4)#This fails 2x-y=1.
~C. (4,1)#This fails x+y=5.
=D. (2,3)#Correct. Adding equivalent work gives x=2 and y=3.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Which point solves y=x² and y=x+6?
{
=A. (3,9)#Correct. 3²=9 and 3+6=9.
~B. (2,8)#2² is 4, not 8.
~C. (0,6)#0² is 0, not 6.
~D. (6,12)#6² is 36, not 12.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=x² and y=9.
{
~A. (9,81)#This is on the parabola but not y=9.
=B. (-3,9) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x²=9.
~C. (0,9)#This is not on y=x².
~D. (3,3)#This is not on y=9.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
How many solutions can a line and a parabola have?
{
~A. Always 1#A line can intersect a parabola in different ways.
~B. Always 2#Tangency or no intersection are possible.
=C. 0, 1, or 2#Correct. A line can miss, touch, or cross a parabola twice.
~D. Exactly 0#Intersections can occur.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
What must be true for (a,b) to solve a system?
{
~A. It satisfies at least one equation.#One equation is not enough.
~B. It is always an x-intercept.#System solutions are not always x-intercepts.
~C. It has positive coordinates only.#Solutions can include negative coordinates.
=D. It satisfies every equation in the system.#Correct. A system solution must make all equations true.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=4 and y=2x.
{
=A. (2,4)#Correct. Set 4=2x, so x=2 and y=4.
~B. (4,2)#This reverses coordinates.
~C. (0,4)#This is on y=4 but not y=2x.
~D. (2,2)#This is on y=2x but not y=4.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=-x+5 and y=x-1.
{
~A. (2,3)#This reverses the solution.
=B. (3,2)#Correct. Set -x+5=x-1, so x=3 and y=2.
~C. (1,4)#This satisfies only the first equation.
~D. (4,1)#This satisfies only the second equation.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
If two lines are parallel and distinct, how many solutions does the system have?
{
~A. One solution#One solution requires one intersection.
~B. Two solutions#Two distinct lines cannot intersect twice.
=C. No solution#Correct. Parallel distinct lines do not intersect.
~D. Infinitely many solutions#That happens when the lines are the same.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
If two equations graph the same line, how many solutions are there?
{
~A. No solution#Same lines overlap completely.
~B. Exactly one solution#Same lines have more than one shared point.
~C. Exactly two solutions#Same lines share infinitely many points.
=D. Infinitely many solutions#Correct. Every point on the line satisfies both equations.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Which method is efficient when both equations are solved for y?
{
=A. Substitution by setting the expressions equal#Correct. Both expressions equal y, so they equal each other.
~B. Ignoring one equation#Both equations are needed.
~C. Only testing x=0#One point may not solve the system.
~D. Changing signs randomly#Algebraic steps must preserve equivalence.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=x+4 and y=10.
{
~A. (10,6)#This reverses coordinates.
=B. (6,10)#Correct. Set x+4=10, so x=6.
~C. (4,10)#This gives y=8 in the first equation.
~D. (6,6)#This is not on y=10.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=x²-4 and y=0.
{
~A. (0,-4)#This is on the parabola but not y=0.
~B. (4,0)#This does not satisfy x²-4=0.
=C. (-2,0) and (2,0)#Correct. Set x²-4=0.
~D. No solution#The parabola crosses y=0 twice.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
What does a solution mean in a revenue-cost system?
{
~A. The maximum revenue always#Intersections do not always indicate maxima.
~B. The cost y-intercept only#A solution is not only an intercept.
~C. A point that satisfies neither model#That is not a system solution.
=D. The break-even point where revenue equals cost#Correct. At the intersection, the two modeled quantities are equal.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve x+y=8 and y=2x-1.
{
=A. (3,5)#Correct. Substitute y=2x-1 into x+y=8 to get 3x=9.
~B. (5,3)#This reverses coordinates and fails y=2x-1.
~C. (2,6)#This satisfies x+y=8 but not y=2x-1.
~D. (4,4)#This fails y=2x-1.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve y=3x and y=x².
{
~A. (1,3)#This is on y=3x but not the parabola.
=B. (0,0) and (3,9)#Correct. Set 3x=x², so x(x-3)=0.
~C. (3,3)#This is not on y=3x.
~D. No solution#The graphs intersect at two points.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Which point solves y=x+5 and y=2x+1?
{
~A. (9,4)#This reverses coordinates.
~B. (1,6)#This satisfies first equation only.
=C. (4,9)#Correct. Set x+5=2x+1, so x=4 and y=9.
~D. (5,10)#This fails the second equation.
}


::ALG2_U05_L01_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2
Question:
Solve 3x+y=10 and x-y=2.
{
~A. (1,3)#This fails x-y=2.
~B. (2,4)#This fails both equations.
~C. (4,-1)#This fails 3x+y=10.
=D. (3,1)#Correct. From x-y=2, y=x-2; substitute to get 4x-2=10.
}

